  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **charge** $C$ of a phone battery **decreases** at a rate that's **inversely proportional** to the **time** $t$ spent charging the smartphone.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *charge* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ is *inversely proportional to* the *time.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\u221d\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $\u221d$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the number *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{t}.\n$$",
